  "intro": {
    "text": "A **slice** cuts a piece out of a string. Put two positions inside the brackets with a colon between them:\n\n```\nsong = \"sunflower\"\nprint(song[0:3])\nprint(song[3:9])\n```\n\nprints sun and then flower. `song[start:stop]` starts **at** start and stops **just before** stop. The stop position is never included, so the piece has `stop - start` characters.\n\n- Leave out start and it begins at 0: `song[:3]` is sun.\n- Leave out stop and it goes to the end: `song[3:]` is flower.\n- Negative positions work too: `song[-5:]` is the last five characters.\n- A third number is the **step**: `song[::2]` takes every second character.\n- A step of -1 walks backwards, so `song[::-1]` is the whole string reversed.\n\nA slice never stops the program with an IndexError. Positions past the end are cut to fit, and if nothing is inside you get an empty string `\"\"`. The **cells** panel lights up every box the slice takes.",
    "analogy": "A window you slide over the row of boxes: the left edge sits on start and the right edge sits just before stop. Everything you can see through the window is the slice."
  },
